{"name":"Velvet Robe","hex":"#939dcc","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"velvet","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A closely woven fabric (originally of silk, now also of cotton or man-made fibres) with a thick short pile on one side.","Very fine fur, including the skin and fur on a deer's antlers."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cover with velvet or with a covering of a similar texture.","To coat raw meat in starch, then in oil, preparatory to frying."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/velvet"},{"term":"robe","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A long loose outer garment, often signifying honorary stature.","The skin of an animal, especially the bison, dressed with the fur on, and used as a wrap."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To clothe; to dress.","To put on official vestments."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/robe"}],"entries":[{"title":"Velvet","blocks":[{"text":"Velvet is a type of woven fabric with a dense, even pile that gives it a distinctive soft feel. Historically, velvet was typically made from silk. Modern velvet can be made from silk, linen, cotton, wool, synthetic fibers, silk-cotton blends, or synthetic-natural fiber blends."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Blue_velvet_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Blue_velvet_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Velvet"},{"title":"Robe","blocks":[{"text":"A robe is a loose-fitting outer garment. Unlike garments described as capes or cloaks, robes usually have sleeves. The English word robe derives from Middle English robe (\"garment\"), borrowed from Old French robe, itself taken from the Frankish word *rouba, and is related to the word rob."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/LongPao.JPG/330px-LongPao.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"http